      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat: clarify iOS Location Always permission flow",
        "body": "Related: #99229\nRelated: #86217\nRelated closed candidate: #99235\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nResolves a problem where iOS users could select Location Always in OpenClaw without the app clearly explaining whether iOS had actually granted background-capable location access. That made wake-up/location workflows ambiguous when iOS only allowed While Using, denied access, global Location Services were disabled, or the user changed OpenClaw/iOS location settings independently.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis adds an explicit iOS permission summary that separates the user's selected OpenClaw mode from the effective iOS authorization state. It also wires the background-location contract into the native configuration and location owner path:\n\n- Background and significant-change monitoring require both desired Always and effective iOS Always authorization.\n- OpenClaw Location Off remains sticky even when iOS still grants location.\n- Slow iOS permission sheets no longer race the prior 1.5-second no-prompt fallback.\n- Stale async Settings refreshes cannot overwrite newer authorization state.\n- External iOS downgrades stop significant-change monitoring, and a restored Always grant restarts it without waiting for an unrelated gateway reconnect.\n\nThis follows the current SettingsProTab pattern because #99231 may reshape the Settings UI. #99235 explored a similar area but was closed after expanding beyond the Location Always flow; this PR stays bounded to location permission state, background/significant-change behavior, native i18n, and focused regression coverage.\n\n## User Impact\n\nUsers get accurate feedback when Location Always is selected but iOS has not granted background-capable permission. Desired Always is preserved through the mismatch instead of silently reverting. Off remains an app-level gate. Monitoring now follows both explicit app intent and effective iOS authorization across in-app requests and later iOS Settings changes.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Exact repaired PR head: `31ddea5efa60d6658c002c16d23bf9a81c36b693`.\n- Current-head consent proof for preserving Off/While Using under broader iOS grants: https://github.com/PollyBot13/openclaw/blob/proof/pr-99247-location-always/proof/pr-99247/current-head-consent-sync-proof.md\n- Physical iPhone before/after flow summary and contact sheet: https://github.com/PollyBot13/openclaw/blob/proof/pr-99247-location-always/proof/pr-99247/final-physical-video-summary.md\n- Redacted physical proof artifact and coverage map: https://github.com/PollyBot13/openclaw/blob/proof/pr-99247-location-always/proof/pr-99247/README.md\n- Focused simulator tests: LocationPermissionSummaryTests plus SwiftUIRenderSmokeTests, 28 passed on iPhone 17 / iOS 26.5. Coverage includes sticky Off, desired/effective mismatches, initial Always monitoring activation, external downgrade, and restored-Always monitoring restart.\n- Permanent UI regression: `OpenClawSnapshotUITests.testLocationAlwaysWaitsForSlowSystemPermissionResponse` passed on iPhone 17 / iOS 26.5. It resets authorization, holds both real system permission sheets open for three seconds, verifies Always and Requesting remain stable, grants Change to Always Allow, and verifies effective background permission. The xcresult preserves full-screen screenshots for the first prompt, upgrade prompt, and final granted state.\n- Native i18n sync/check: `entries=2460 changed=false` after sync.\n- SwiftFormat lint: clean on all repaired Swift files. `git diff --check`: clean.\n- Whole-branch autoreview found one P1 transition defect: restored Always authorization did not restart significant-change monitoring until reconnect. The accepted owner-level repair added authorization-change reconciliation and regression coverage. Fresh focused autoreview then returned no findings, patch correct, confidence 0.94.\n- Built simulator Info.plist includes `UIBackgroundModes = [audio, location, remote-notification]`.\n\nKnown proof boundary: contributor physical-device evidence predates the final prompt-wait/monitor-restart repairs. The repaired exact head has deterministic simulator coverage and screenshots, but no fresh physical-device terminated-app significant-change relaunch trace because no device is currently attached.\n\nAI-assisted: yes, Codex.\n",
